# WHAT THE CAMERA ACTUALLY DOES INDOORS — measured before anything is designed.
#
# A house, a door in it, and the author standing in the middle of the room. The
# boom already sweeps walls, so the question is not "does it know" but "what does
# it do when the room is smaller than the boom's own minimum".

echo --- flat ground
at 0 0 0
key ArrowUp
wait rebuilt

echo --- a house where the author is looking
at 0 -4 90
key H
wait rebuilt

echo --- from outside, for reference — the control the indoor rows are read against
at 0 -10 90
step 90
snap s10-house-outside
# ⚠ THE CONTROL FOR THE EYE, AND IT IS THE ROW THAT MAKES THE OTHER TWO MEAN
# ANYTHING. Outdoors nothing obstructs the boom, so the eye sits a full `want`
# behind the character — and this row is INSENSITIVE to the fault the other two
# catch, which is what a control is for.
#
# ⚠ IT READ 5.900 UNTIL `boom_take` STOPPED CHARGING A SKIN IT DID NOT OWE. The
# march returns `want` when it finds nothing, and the skin was subtracted anyway —
# so the open-field boom was `want - skin` for as long as this has existed. This
# band is what an UNOBSTRUCTED boom measures, and the old one was the bug's value.
cam 5.9 6.3
# The control, judged: outdoors the camera works, so these thresholds are what
# "works" measures as — the subject at least half a percent of the frame, and no
# single surface over 60% of it.
#
# ⚠ AND THE SOFFIT'S CONTROL IS *ABSENCE*, which is the strongest row here. The
# roof's underside faces the room, so from the road it is behind the roof at every
# pixel — if it shows outdoors it has been built on the wrong side of the tent,
# which is precisely what a backwards face normal does. Measured: 0.0001.
frame 0.005 0.60 soffit 0 0.005

# ⚠ THE BOOM IS EASED, NOT SNAPPED — deliberately, because a boom that stepped in
# one tick was "correct, and clearly a mechanism rather than a camera". So `at` and
# `snap` in the same breath photograph the camera the author LEFT, not the one the
# room gives them. The first version of this script did exactly that and reported no
# change from a fix that was working.
echo --- trace the camera's own numbers, not the milliseconds
send 27:1

# ⚠ THE MODE IS PINNED, BECAUSE THE ROWS BELOW ARE FOLLOW'S. The design's own
# measurement table is PER MODE — "the modes disagree by design and a single
# expectation would be wrong for two of them" — and AUTO would leave these two
# stations in FOLLOW today only because `sh_room` happens to be over the threshold
# there. A gate that depends on where a threshold sits is a gate that goes red when
# the threshold is tuned, for no defect.
send 40:1

echo --- and standing in the middle of its floor
at 0 0 90
step 90
snap s10-house-inside
# The boom collapsed to the room less its skin — and, the part that was broken,
# the collapse REACHED THE WIRE. This read 5.900 while the trace said 1.87.
cam 1.5 3.0
# ⚠ WHAT IS OVERHEAD IS THE CEILING, NOT THE OUTSIDE OF THE ROOF. Nothing culls
# backfaces here, so before the soffit existed the interior looked up at the roof's
# own triangles, lit by an upward normal — the same shade as the tiles, seen from
# below. `roof` near zero and `soffit` a fifth of the frame is that, as pixels.
frame 0.005 0.60 soffit 0.10 1.0 roof 0 0.05 sky 0 0.001

echo --- facing a corner, where the boom has least room
at 0 0 225
step 90
snap s10-house-corner
# Along the diagonal the room is longer, so the boom keeps more of itself — the
# opposite direction from the row above, which is why both are here.
cam 3.5 5.2
frame 0.005 0.60 soffit 0.10 1.0 roof 0 0.05 sky 0 0.001

# ── AUTO DEGRADING INTO SNUG ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ THE PAIR IS THE CLAIM, NOT EITHER ROW. A single SNUG frame proves nothing: the
# failure mode of a close camera is a picture with nothing in it, and "dark" and
# "empty" are indistinguishable to anyone but the person who wrote it. Two stations
# on one floor, either side of the threshold, is what says the mode did something.
echo --- AUTO, and the middle of the floor is over the threshold
send 40:0
at 0 0 90
step 120
snap s10-auto-follow
# The control: AUTO has NOT degraded here, so this must read as FOLLOW does.
# `lum` is the frame's mean luminance — the classifier matches on chromaticity and
# is blind to a scalar by construction, so the ambient is the one thing it cannot
# see and this is the number that can.
frame 0.005 0.60 figure 0.02 1.0 lum 0.24 1.0 sd 0 0.115 sky 0 0.001

echo --- AUTO, close to the wall, where sh_room 0.88 says a boom does not fit
at 0 -1.5 90
step 120
snap s10-auto-snug
# ⚠ THREE CLAIMS, AND THE MIDDLE ONE IS THE ONE THIS FILE EARNED. The figure is
# gone, which is what SNUG is for. The light is DOWN, which is the only thing the
# ambient changes in a picture. And no single surface may take 85% — "unconstrained,
# pressing in is the point" was the design's row, and it let a frame through that was
# 99.7% ONE WALL FACE with the lens inside the masonry. Pressing in is not the same
# as being buried, and a bound this loose forbids only the second.
# ⚠ THE FIGURE BOUND IS 0.002 AND NOT ZERO, AND THAT IS THE INSTRUMENT'S FAULT
# RATHER THAN THE CAMERA'S. Anti-aliased pixels along the FLOOR/WALL seam blend
# 0.500/0.308/0.192 with 0.359/0.340/0.301, and the midpoint of those two is
# 0.43/0.32/0.25 — which is the SUBJECT's chromaticity to three decimals. So the
# figure bucket collects the seam, and the residue scales with how much seam is in
# frame, not with anything about the character. Measured: 0.0003 here against
# 0.1364 at the station above, a factor of 450. The bound is set above the seam and
# far below a body.
# `sd` is the whole frame's contrast: SNUG is darker than FOLLOW *and* higher
# contrast than it, which is the design's *"a black frame is not atmosphere"* made
# numeric — a black frame and a flat grey one both have a spread of zero.
#
# ⚠ BUT `sd` CANNOT SEE THE LAMP, and that took three measurements to accept.
# Dropping the ambient widens the SAME histogram, so lamp-on and lamp-off at the
# same ambient read 0.1415 and 0.1418. `sd:masonry` is the spread WITHIN one
# surface, and that is the lamp's signature: with only an ambient and one
# directional light a wall is ONE plane with ONE normal and ONE colour, so every
# pixel of it has the identical luminance. A light that falls off with distance is
# the only term that can make it vary. Measured at this station: **0.0031 without
# the lamp, 0.0762 with it** — a factor of 25, against a whole-frame `sd` that moved
# by 0.0003.
#
# ⚠ It is a claim about THIS STATION. `masonry` merges wall and frame, whose base
# colours differ, so a view with a door surround in it spreads the bucket for a
# reason that is not the lamp — FOLLOW mid-floor reads 0.0507 either way. The 0.0031
# baseline here is what says no frame is in this view.
# ⚠ AND `black` IS THE DESIGN'S OWN ROW, LITERALLY. *"A black frame is not
# atmosphere"* — the classifier already buckets anything under a luminance floor as
# `black`, and with the lamp switched off at this ambient **2.35% of the frame is
# exactly that**, with a further third of it fallen so dark that a WALL now matches
# the ROAD's chromaticity. The lamp is what keeps a low-ambient room a picture
# rather than a silhouette, and this is the number that says so.
frame 0 0.85 figure 0 0.002 lum 0.05 0.22 sd 0.125 1.0 sd:masonry 0.03 1.0 black 0 0.002 sky 0 0.001

echo --- and back out: the degradation is not a one-way door
at 0 0 90
step 120
frame 0.005 0.60 figure 0.02 1.0 lum 0.24 1.0 sd 0 0.115 sky 0 0.001

# ── CUTAWAY — seeing a plan while you build it ───────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ THE SAME STANDING POSITION AS THE FOLLOW ROW ABOVE. CUTAWAY is not "a nicer
# picture"; it is *the same author, in the same place, able to see what they are
# editing*. So the difference has to be in the frame and not in where they walked.
echo --- CUTAWAY, from the exact spot FOLLOW showed a wall and a ceiling
send 40:3
at 0 0 90
step 150
snap s10-cutaway
# ⚠ `soffit` IS THE DECISIVE ROW AND IT IS WHY THE ROOF BECAME TWO SURFACES.
# FOLLOW at this spot reads soffit 0.240 and roof 0.011; CUTAWAY takes BOTH, which
# could not have been expressed at all while they shared one mesh — SNUG keeps the
# underside and CUTAWAY removes it, from the same geometry.
#
# `grass` is the claim that says it worked: standing INDOORS, a tenth of the frame
# is the field outside. That is only possible with the roof off AND the eye above
# the wall head, which is the whole of stage 1.
#
# The subject is small on purpose — "drawn, small" in the mode table — so the bound
# is 0.002 rather than FOLLOW's 0.005: this row asks whether the figure is THERE,
# for scale, not whether it is the subject.
frame 0.002 0.60 soffit 0 0.001 roof 0 0.001 grass 0.10 1.0

echo --- and back to AUTO: the roof returns, which no other row would notice
send 40:0
at 0 0 90
step 150
frame 0.005 0.60 soffit 0.10 1.0 grass 0 0.001

# ── EYES — being in the room, and the row the whole design ordered itself around ──
#
# ⚠ *"Looking straight up, sky pixels: 0 (today: all of them)"* is the design's own
# measurement row, written when the roof had no underside — which is exactly why EYES
# was last on the list. Until a ceiling existed, a first-person view indoors was a
# picture of the sky through it. This row is the soffit's payoff.
echo --- EYES, OUTDOORS, looking straight up: the control
send 40:4
at 0 -10 90
send 3:0,-20000
step 120
# ⚠ THE CONTROL IS THE POINT. "No sky" is only a result if the instrument can find
# sky when there is some — otherwise a camera drawing nothing scores the same.
frame 0 1.0 sky 0.99 1.0

echo --- EYES, INDOORS, looking straight up: a ceiling, and no sky at all
at 0 0 90
step 120
snap s10-eyes-up
frame 0 1.0 sky 0 0.001 soffit 0.5 1.0

echo --- EYES, indoors, level: a room seen from inside a head, and no body in it
send 3:0,250
step 120
snap s10-eyes-level
frame 0 0.85 figure 0 0.002 sky 0 0.001 sd 0.02 1.0

echo --- back to AUTO with the pitch still where EYES left it
send 3:0,-20000
send 40:0
step 150
# ⚠ THE ROW THAT CATCHES A FENCE APPLIED ONLY AT THE INPUT. EYES allows ±1.5 and
# FOLLOW allows −0.20; leaving one for the other used to carry the out-of-fence value
# across, because the clamp lived in the LOOK handler and a mode change is not a look.
# A normal FOLLOW frame here — the body back, a wall ahead — is the whole assertion.
frame 0.005 0.60 figure 0.02 1.0 soffit 0.05 1.0

